/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (1:00\2052:00\012\012The activity uses original sources and puts their limits beside the findings. WHO reported signs of problematic social media use in eleven percent of the young people surveyed in 2022. The study covered almost 280,000 participants aged eleven, thirteen, and fifteen across forty-four countries and regions. That gives useful population context. It cannot diagnose one person or define a safe number of minutes for everyone. UNESCO's media literacy resources offer another kind of evidence: guidance for thinking critically about information. Guidance can help us decide how to check a post, but it does not establish whether that post is true.\012\012Presenter cue: Point out the difference between a population finding and educational guidance.\012\012WHO, published 25 September 2024: https://www.who.int/europe/news/item/25-09-2024-teens--screens-and-mental-health\012UNESCO, Media and Information Literacy, checked 4 October 2026: https://www.unesco.org/en/media-information-literacy) /QuadPoints [ 1234 12 1254 12 1234 32 1254 32 ] /Rect [ 1234 12 1254 32 ] /Subtype /Text /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
