/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (4:00\2055:00\012\012In Evaluate, the relevant sources help me think about checking information. I can see the publisher and date, then open the population and limitation section. UNESCO's guidance asks me to engage critically with information. It does not verify our fictional transport claim. I would still need a dated announcement from the transit agency, with the details and conditions. This is why comparing a finding with its limits matters. A trustworthy source can guide a process without answering every question about a particular post.\012\012Presenter cue: Live: choose Next step. Expand Who it covers & what it cannot tell us on the UNESCO literacy card. Read the evidence response. Backup: point to the expanded source card in the screenshot.\012\012UNESCO: https://www.unesco.org/en/media-information-literacy\012UNESCO generative AI guidance: https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/guidance-generative-ai-education-and-research) /QuadPoints [ 1234 12 1254 12 1234 32 1254 32 ] /Rect [ 1234 12 1254 32 ] /Subtype /Text /Type /Annot
>>
